    <title>Questions</title>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q1">
<statement>
<p>
The length of a rectangle is increasing at a rate of 8 cm/sec and its width
is increasing at a rate of 3 cm/sec. When the length is 20 cm and the width
is 10 cm, how fast is the area of the rectangle increasing?
</p>
<figure xml:id="myrectanglefall2024_ex3">
  <caption>
A rectangle of width W and length L.  
  </caption>
  <image xml:id="myrectanglefall2024ex3pic" width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thick]
\draw[color=linecolor, line width=1.5pt] (0,0) -- (6,0) -- (6,3) -- (0,3) -- (0,0) node [above] at (3,3) {$L$};
\draw[color=linecolor] node [left] at (0,1.5) {$W$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Let <m>A(t)=L(t)W(t)</m>.  Then
<md>
\frac{dA}{dt}
=
L\frac{dW}{dt}
+
W\frac{dL}{dt}.
</md>
Substituting the given values,
<md>
\frac{dA}{dt}
=
20(3)+10(8)
=
140.
</md>
The area is increasing at
<m>140~\text{cm}^2/\text{sec}</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise> 

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q2">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose the derivative of a function <m>f</m> is given by
</p>
<md>
f'(x)=(x-2)^2(x+4).
</md>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Use a first-derivative sign chart to determine the intervals on which
<m>f</m> is increasing.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Does <m>f</m> have any local maxima? If so, where?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Does <m>f</m> have any local minima? If so, where?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The critical numbers are
<m>x=-4</m> and <m>x=2</m>.
</p>
<p>
Since <m>(x-2)^2 \ge 0</m>, the sign of <m>f'(x)</m> is determined by
<m>x+4</m>.
</p>
<p>
Therefore:
</p>
<ul>
<li><m>f'(x) &lt; 0</m> on <m>(-\infty,-4)</m>.</li>
<li><m>f'(x) &gt; 0</m> on <m>(-4,2)</m>.</li>
<li><m>f'(x) &gt; 0</m> on <m>(2,\infty)</m>.</li>
</ul>
<p>
Hence <m>f</m> is increasing on <m>
(-4,2)\cup(2,\infty).
</m>
</p>
<p>
There is no local maximum because the derivative never changes from
positive to negative.
</p>
<p>
There is a local minimum at
<m>x=-4</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q3">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<m>
f(x)=\frac{\ln x}{x}.
</m>
</p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>State the domain of <m>f</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate
<m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^+}\frac{\ln x}{x}</m>.  Defend your response.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Determine the maximum value attained by <m>f</m> and where it occurs.  Again, defend your response.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a point of inflection at <m>x=e^p</m> where <m>p=\underline{\hspace{10mm}}</m>.  
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Domain:
<m>(0,\infty).</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<md>
\lim_{x\to0^+}\frac{\ln x}{x}
=
-\infty.
</md>
</li>
<li>
  <p>
Differentiate:
</p>
<md>
f'(x)
=
\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}.
</md>
<p>
Critical number:
</p>
<md>
1-\ln x=0
\implies
x=e.
</md>
<p>
Since <m>f'</m> changes from positive to negative at
<m>x=e</m>,
<m>f</m> attains an absolute maximum there.
</p>
<md>
f(e)
=
\frac{1}{e}.
</md>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Differentiate again:
</p>
<md>
f''(x)
=
\frac{-3+2\ln x}{x^3}.
</md>
<p>
Setting the numerator equal to zero gives
</p>
<md>
-3+2\ln x=0.
</md>
<md>
\ln x=\frac32.
</md>
<md>
x=e^{3/2}.
</md>
</li>
</ol>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q4">
<statement>
<p>
A car traveling along a straight road is braking and its velocity is
measured at several different points in time, as recorded in the table.
</p>
<tabular top="major" bottom="major">
<row>
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell>0</cell>
<cell>0.4</cell>
<cell>0.8</cell>
<cell>1.5</cell>
<cell>2.0</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>v(t)</m></cell>
<cell>100</cell>
<cell>88</cell>
<cell>72</cell>
<cell>54</cell>
<cell>0</cell>
</row>
</tabular>

<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Estimate the total distance traveled during the time the car brakes using a right Riemann sum
<m>R_4</m> with 4 subintervals.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
Assuming that <m>v(t)</m> is always decreasing on
<m>[0,2]</m>, what is the maximum possible distance the car traveled before it stopped?  Why?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</statement>

<answer>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<m>
R_4
=
(0.4)(88)+(0.4)(72)+(0.7)(54)+(0.5)(0)
=
101.8.
</m>

<p>
Approximate distance:
<m>101.8</m> feet.
</p>
</li>
<li> We use a left sum to find an upper bound:
<m>
L_4
=
(0.4)(100)
+(0.4)(88)
+(0.7)(72)
+(0.5)(54)
=
152.6.
</m>
<p>
Maximum possible distance:
<m>152.6</m> feet.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q5">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the value of each limit below or state that it does not exist.  Be sure to defend your answers.  
</p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<m>\displaystyle 
\lim_{t\to1}
\frac{nt^n-(n+1)t^{n-1}+1}{2(t-1)}.
</m>
</li>

<li>
<m>\displaystyle
\lim_{x\to{-\frac{\pi}{2}^+}}
\frac{x+\frac{\pi}{2}}{\tan(x)}.
</m>
</li>
</ol>
</statement>

<answer>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Since both the numerator and denominator approach 0, we apply L'Hôpital's Rule:
<md><mrow>
\lim_{t\to1}
\frac{nt^n-(n+1)t^{n-1}+1}{2(t-1)} \mathstrut \amp = \lim_{t\to1} \frac{n^2t^{n-1} - (n-1)(n+1)t^{n-2}}{2} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \frac{n^2 - (n^2-1)}{2} </mrow>
<mrow> \amp =\frac{1}{2}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>
L'Hôpital's Rule is not applicable since the numerator gets close to 0 while the denominator gets close to <m>-\infty</m>.  The limit is simply approaching 0.  
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q6">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the left Riemann sum <m>\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{3} f(x_k)\Delta x</m>
on the interval <m>[1,7]</m> where <m>\Delta x=2</m>.  The graph of <m>f</m> passes through
<m>(1,2)</m>,
<m>(3,5)</m>,
<m>(5,7)</m>,
and
<m>(7,8)</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="Riemannsumfall2024_ex3">
  <caption>
The graph of a function passing through <m>(1,2), (3,5), (5,7)</m> and <m>(7,8)</m>. 
  </caption>
  <image xml:id="Riemannsumfall2024ex3pic" width="50%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thick,scale=1]
\draw[step=1cm,color=black,dotted] (0,-1) grid (5,3);
   \draw [black!80,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (5,0) node [below] at (4.5,0) {$x$};
   \draw [black!80,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (-0.5,0);
   \draw [black!80,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (0,3) node [right] at (0,2.75) {$y$};
   \draw [black!80,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (0,0) -- (0,-0.5);
\draw[color=linecolor, line width=1pt,domain=0.5:3.5]   plot (\x,{-1/32*(4*\x*\x)+1/2*(2*\x)+1/32}) node [above] at (1.65,2) {$\displaystyle f(x)$};
\fill [black] (0.5,2/4) circle(2pt);
\fill [black] (1.5,5/4) circle(2pt);
\fill [black] (2.5,7/4) circle(2pt);
\fill [black] (3.5,8/4) circle(2pt);
\foreach \x/\xtext in { 1/2, 2/4, 3/6, 4/8}
    \draw[shift={(\x,0)}] (0pt,3pt) -- (0pt,-3pt) node[below] {$\xtext$};
\foreach \x/\xtext in {0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75, 2, 2.25, 2.5, 2.75, 3, 3.25, 3.5, 3.75, 4}
    \draw[shift={(\x,0)}] (0pt,1.5pt) -- (0pt,-1.5pt);
\foreach \y/\ytext in {1/4, 2/8}
    \draw[shift={(0,\y)}] (-2pt,0pt) -- (2pt,0pt) node[left] {$\ytext$};
    \end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</statement>
<answer>
<m>
L_3
=
2(2)+2(5)+2(7)
=
28.
</m>
<figure xml:id="RiemannsumAnsfall2024_ex3">
  <caption>
The left Riemann sum for <m>f</m>. 
  </caption>
  <image xml:id="RiemannsumAnsfall2024ex3pic" width="50%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thick,scale=1]
\fill [linecolor!20] (1/2,0)--(1/2,2/4)--(3/2,2/4)--(3/2,0)--(1/2,0);
\fill [linecolor!20] (3/2,0)--(3/2,5/4)--(5/2,5/4)--(5/2,0)--(3/2,0);
\fill [linecolor!20] (5/2,0)--(5/2,7/4)--(7/2,7/4)--(7/2,0)--(5/2,0);
\draw [linecolor] (1/2,0)--(1/2,2/4)--(3/2,2/4)--(3/2,0)--(1/2,0);
