    \end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q7">
<statement>
<p>Evaluate each expression.</p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li><m>\displaystyle \sum_{k=3}^{10}4</m></li>
<li>
<m>\displaystyle \sum_{m=1}^{200}m^3 - \sum_{m=1}^{199}m^3
</m>
</li>
<li>
<m>
\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{4}(-1)^k k^k
</m>
</li>
</ol>
</statement>

<answer>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<md>(4+4+4+\ldots+4)_{\underbrace{\text{8 times}}}=8(4)=32.</md>
</li>
<li>
<md>
\sum_{m=1}^{200}5m^3
=
200^3 = 8,000,000.
</md>
</li>
<li>
<md>
(-1)^1\cdot 1^1 + (-1)^2\cdot 2^2 + (-1)^3\cdot 3^3 + (-1)^4\cdot 4^4 = -1 + 4 -27+256 = 232
</md>
</li>
</ol>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q8">
<statement>
<p>
    Consider all rectangles (two examples appear here) which have two sides on
    the positive coordinate axes and which lie under the curve
    <m>y=2\cos(x)</m>.
    The one with the largest <em>perimeter</em> has width <m>\underline{\hspace{10mm}}</m> and height
    <m>\underline{\hspace{10mm}}</m>.
</p>

  <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
    <figure xml:id="F24Ex2q8a">
  <caption>One example of a rectangle having sides defined by the positive coordinate axes and the curve <m>y=\cos(x)</m>.</caption> 
  <image source="images/MaximumSizeRectangleA.jpg" width="100%">
    </image>
</figure>
    <figure xml:id="F24Ex2q8b">
  <caption>A second example of a rectangle having sides defined by the positive coordinate axes and the curve <m>y=\cos(x)</m>.</caption> 
  <image source="images/MaximumSizeRectangleB.jpg" width="100%">
   </image>
</figure>
  </sidebyside>
      
</statement>
<answer>
  <p>
    Define the perimeter in terms of <m>x</m> which is the length of the base of the rectangle by <md>P(x)=2x+4\cos(x).</md>  Then, <md>P'(x)=2-4\sin(x) = 0</md> exactly when <m>\sin(x) = \frac{1}{2}</m>.  This first happens for positive <m>x</m>-values when <m>x=\frac{\pi}{6}</m> radians.  A first-derivative sign chart shows that <md>P'(x) &gt; 0 \text{ for } 0 &lt; x &lt; \frac{\pi}{6}</md>  and <md>P'(x) &lt; 0 \text{ for } \frac{\pi}{6} &lt; x &lt; \frac{\pi}{2}.</md>  The following table shows that the maximum perimeter occurs when <m>x=\frac{\pi}{6}</m> which occurs when the height is <m>2\cos(\frac{\pi}{6}) = 2\cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = \sqrt{3}.</m> 
  </p>
  <tabular>
  <col halign = "center" right="minor"/>
  <col halign = "center" />
  <row top="major" bottom="major">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>P(x)</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
<cell><m>2\frac{\pi}{6} + 4\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \approx 4.51</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
<cell><m>2\frac{\pi}{2} + 4\cos(\frac{\pi}{2}) = \pi</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exam3-f24-q9">
<statement>
The <em>maximum</em> value of <m>f(x)=\sqrt{x} + \frac{4}{x}</m> on <m>\left[ \frac{1}{4}, 100 \right]</m> occurs at <m>x= \underline{\hspace{10mm}}.</m>  The maximum value of <m>f</m> attained on this interval is <m>\underline{\hspace{10mm}}</m>.  
</statement>
<answer>
  <p>
    <md>f'(x)= \frac{1}{2}x^{-\frac{1}{2}} - 4x^{-2} = 0 </md>
    <md> \iff \frac{1}{2}x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 4 = 0 </md>
    <md> \iff x^{\frac{3}{2}} = 8 </md>
    <md> \iff x^{\frac{1}{2}} = 2 </md>
    <md> \iff x = 4. </md>
    Setting up a table and checking endpoints and critical numbers tells us that the maximum value occurs at <m>x=\frac{1}{4}</m> and the maximum value of <m>f</m> attained on the interval is 16.5.  
  </p>
  <tabular>
  <col halign = "center" right="minor"/>
  <col halign = "center" />
  <row top="major" bottom="major">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></cell>
<cell><m>\frac{1}{2} + 16 = 16.5</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>2+1=3</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>100</m></cell>
<cell><m>10+0.04 = 10.04</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</answer>
</exercise>


</exercises>

</section>
